gh-flaring or leaky sources. So replacing some conventional oil with unconventional oil could actually reduce net emissions in the near term, buying us time to scale renewables. Finally, let me address the heart of your concern: “How can we be sure?” The honest answer is that we can’t be 100% sure—no policy decision comes with a guarantee. But the risk of lock-in from unconventional oil is lower than the risk of energy shortages, economic instability, and slowed renewable investment if we cut off supply too abruptly. The worst-case scenario for lock-in is that we overbuild slightly and have to retire some assets early—a cost we can absorb. The worst-case scenario for halting development is that we face energy poverty, political backlash, and a slower transition overall. So I’m not saying there’s no risk. I’m saying that when you compare the two paths—developing unconventional oil with strong environmental safeguards, versus stopping development entirely—the former gives us more flexibility, more time, and better odds of actually reaching our climate goals. That’s why I believe it’s justified.
