sk. However, I'd argue that the alternative—not developing unconventional oil at all—doesn't eliminate that risk; it just shifts it. If we stop developing unconventional oil, the world will likely turn to other sources: conventional oil from politically unstable regions with weaker environmental regulations, or even coal and heavy oil from places like Venezuela or the Middle East. Those sources can have even higher lifecycle emissions and fewer opportunities for methane control or CCS. So the comparison isn't between unconventional oil and a clean utopia; it's between unconventional oil and other fossil fuels that are often dirtier and harder to regulate. Finally, I want to be honest about uncertainty. I can't guarantee that methane leaks will be fixed or that CCS will scale. But I can say that the path forward isn't to bet everything on one outcome—it's to build a system with multiple layers of protection. That means: (1) strong regulations on methane, (2) investment in CCS and direct air capture, (3) a rising carbon price that makes cleaner alternatives competitive, and (4) continued aggressive deployment of renewables. If we do all of that, unconventional oil can be part of a responsible transition. If we don't, then no energy source—renewable or fossil—will save us. So I'm not saying the risk is zero. I'm saying that the risk of *not* developing unconventional oil—energy shortages, economic pain, reliance on dirtier fuels, and slower renewable investment—is likely greater. That's why I believe it's justified.
