ortation emissions. Now, you might say: "But we should be replacing fossil fuels with renewables, not with other fossil fuels." And I agree completely—that's the ultimate goal. But we can't skip the transition period. During that period, we need a bridge fuel that is as clean as possible, reliable, and affordable. Unconventional natural gas, in particular, emits about half the CO₂ of coal when burned, and modern production techniques can keep methane leaks low. So using unconventional gas to replace coal plants is actually a *net reduction* in carbon emissions in the near term—even if it's not a perfect solution. The other part of this is that stopping unconventional oil development doesn't just affect emissions—it affects the pace of renewable deployment. Renewables require massive upfront investment, and that investment comes from a healthy economy. If energy prices spike because we cut off supply, economic growth slows, and governments have less money to spend on clean energy subsidies, grid upgrades, and research. We've seen this dynamic play out repeatedly: high energy prices lead to political backlash against climate policy, as happened with the "yellow vest" protests in France and the recent energy crisis in Europe. So when I say developing unconventional oil is the "safer bet," I mean it in this specific sense: it gives us a smoother, more politically sustainable path to decarbonization. It avoids the risk of economic disruption that could derail climate action entirely. It allows us to replace the dirtiest fuels (coal, high-leak conventional oil) with relatively cleaner alternatives. And it buys us time to build the renewable infrastructure we need. I fully understand the instinct to say "no more fossil fuels, period." That's a morally clear and emotionally satisfying position. But I believe the most effective way to stop adding carbon to the atmosphere as soon as possible is to take a pragmatic, phased approach—one that includes unconventional oil as a bridge, not a destination. The worst thing we could do is to pursue a strategy that sounds good but actually slows the transition. That's why I think the development is justified.
