ther places. I also think it's important to recognize that the world has changed since the 1990s. Renewable energy is now cost-competitive with fossil fuels in many markets, even without subsidies. Battery storage is plummeting in cost. Electric vehicles are scaling rapidly. The economic and technological landscape is fundamentally different. So the risk that cheap oil will completely derail the transition is lower today than it was 20 years ago. We're not starting from scratch—we have momentum. Still, I want to be honest: I can't guarantee that strong policies will be enacted. That's a political question, not a technical one. And if I'm wrong—if we develop unconventional oil and the policies never come—then we will have made the problem worse. That's a real possibility. So why do I still think the trade-off is justified? Because I believe the alternative—halting development and hoping that scarcity will force a transition—is even riskier. Scarcity leads to high energy prices, which lead to economic pain, political backlash, and often a return to coal or other dirty fuels. The 2022 energy crisis in Europe is a perfect example: high prices led some countries to burn more coal and delay nuclear phaseouts. Scarcity doesn't automatically drive a clean transition—it often drives panic and regression. In the end, the choice isn't between a perfect path and a risky one. It's between two imperfect, uncertain paths. I lean toward the one that provides stability, buys time, and gives us the resources to invest in the transition—while acknowledging that it requires strong policy to work. If the policies fail, then yes, we'll have kicked the can. But if we stop development and the world turns to coal, we'll have kicked it even further. That's why I believe the development is justified—not because it's a sure bet, but because it's the bet that gives us the best chance of actually reaching our climate goals without breaking our societies in the process.
